            "output": "<path>/Users/aasheesh/Documents/webdev/os/cli/CLAUDE.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Entire - CLI\n2: \n3: This repo contains the CLI for Entire.\n4: \n5: ## Architecture\n6: \n7: - CLI built with github.com/spf13/cobra and github.com/charmbracelet/huh\n8: \n9: ## Key Directories\n10: \n11: ### Commands (`cmd/`)\n12: \n13: - `entire/`: Main CLI entry point. Also home to kubectl-style external-command resolution (`entire <name>` → `entire-<name>` on PATH) — see [External Commands](docs/architecture/external-commands.md).\n14: - `entire/cli`: CLI utilities and helpers (Cobra commands, helpers, group roots)\n15: - `entire/cli/commands`: actual command implementations\n16: - `entire/cli/agent`: agent implementations (Claude Code, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, Pi) - see [Agent Integration Checklist](docs/architecture/agent-integration-checklist.md) and [Agent Implementation Guide](docs/architecture/agent-guide.md)\n17: - `entire/cli/strategy`: strategy implementation (manual-commit) - see section below\n18: - `entire/cli/checkpoint`: checkpoint storage abstractions (temporary and committed)\n19: - `entire/cli/session`: session state management\n20: - `entire/cli/integration_test`: integration tests (simulated hooks)\n21: - `e2e/`: E2E tests with real agent calls (see [e2e/README.md](e2e/README.md))\n22: \n23: ### Command Layout\n24: \n25: The CLI is organized around five noun groups plus a small set of top-level\n26: verbs. The groups are the canonical home for each verb; legacy top-level\n27: shortcuts remain functional but hidden, and emit a deprecation hint pointing\n28: at the canonical group form.\n29: \n30: - `session` (alias: `sessions`): `list`, `info`, `stop`, `attach`, `resume`, `current`\n31: - `checkpoint` (aliases: `cp`, `checkpoints`): `list`, `explain`, `rewind`, `search`\n32: - `agent`: bare opens the interactive agent selector, plus `list`, `add`, `remove`\n33: - `configure`: bare prints help and a hint pointing at `entire agent`; flags\n34:   manage non-agent settings (telemetry, git-hook installation mode, strategy\n35:   options, summary provider). Agent CRUD lives under `entire agent`.\n36: - `auth`: `login`, `logout`, `status`, `list`, `revoke`\n37: - `doctor`: bare runs the scan-and-fix flow, plus `trace`, `logs`, `bundle`\n38: \n39: Top-level lifecycle and standalone commands: `enable`, `disable`, `status`,\n40: `login`, `logout`, `clean`, `version`, `dispatch`, `activity`, `help`,\n41: `configure`.\n42: \n43: Hidden top-level shortcuts (functional, emit a one-line deprecation hint):\n44: `rewind` → `checkpoint rewind`, `resume` → `session resume`, `attach` →\n45: `session attach`, `explain` → `checkpoint explain`, `trace` → `doctor trace`.\n46: Cobra-native aliases (no hint): `sessions` → `session`, `cp`/`checkpoints` →\n47: `checkpoint`. The `search` top-level remains hidden without a hint.\n48: \n49: Deprecated top-level alias (functional, prints cobra deprecation message):\n50: `reset` → `clean`.\n51: \n52: Hidden infrastructure commands: `hooks`, `migrate`, `trail`,\n53: `curl-bash-post-install`, `__send_analytics`.\n54: \n55: The `hideAsAlias(cmd, canonical)` helper in `cmd/entire/cli/aliascmd.go`\n56: marks a command Hidden and sets cobra's `Deprecated` field so the hint\n57: renders to stderr on every invocation while the command stays functional.\n58: Diagnostic subcommands live alongside `doctor.go` as `doctor_logs.go` and\n59: `doctor_bundle.go`. Group roots and noun-group children live in files\n60: named `<noun>_group.go` and `<noun>_<verb>.go` respectively.\n61: \n62: ## Tech Stack\n63: \n64: - Language: Go 1.26.x\n65: - Build tool: mise, go modules\n66: - Linting: golangci-lint\n67: \n68: ## Development\n69: \n70: ### Running Tests\n71: \n72: ```bash\n73: mise run test\n74: ```\n75: \n76: ### Running Integration Tests\n77: \n78: ```bash\n79: mise run test:integration\n80: ```\n81: \n82: ### Running All Tests (CI)\n83: \n84: ```bash\n85: mise run test:ci\n86: ```\n87: \n88: This runs unit tests, integration tests, and the E2E canary (Vogon agent) in sequence. Integration tests use the `//go:build integration` build tag and are located in `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/`.\n89: \n90: ### Running E2E Canary Tests (Vogon Agent)\n91: \n92: The Vogon agent is a deterministic fake agent that exercises the full E2E test suite without making any API calls. Named after the Vogons from The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy — bureaucratic, procedural, and deterministic to a fault.\n93: \n94: ```bash\n95: mise run test:e2e:canary           # Run all E2E tests with the Vogon agent\n96: mise run test:e2e:canary TestFoo   # Run a specific test\n97: ```\n98: \n99: - **Runs as part of `test:ci`** — canary failures block merges\n100: - **No API calls, no cost** — safe to run freely, unlike real agent E2E tests\n101: - **If a canary test fails, the bug is in the CLI or test infrastructure**, not in an agent\n102: - Located in `e2e/vogon/` (binary) and `cmd/entire/cli/agent/vogon/` (Agent interface)\n103: - The binary parses prompts via regex, creates/modifies/deletes files, and fires lifecycle hooks\n104: - **IMPORTANT: When changing E2E test prompt wording**, the Vogon binary (`e2e/vogon/main.go`) parses prompts with hardcoded regexes. New phrasing may not match existing patterns — always run `mise run test:e2e:canary` after changing prompt text and fix Vogon's parsing if tests fail.\n105: \n106: ### Running E2E Tests (Only When Explicitly Requested)\n107: \n108: **IMPORTANT: Do NOT run E2E tests proactively.** E2E tests make real API calls to agents, which consume tokens and cost money. Only run them when the user explicitly asks for E2E testing.\n109: \n110: ```bash\n111: mise run test:e2e [filter]                          # All agents, filtered\n112: mise run test:e2e --agent claude-code [filter]       # Claude Code only\n113: mise run test:e2e --agent gemini-cli [filter]        # Gemini CLI only\n114: mise run test:e2e --agent opencode [filter]          # OpenCode only\n115: mise run test:e2e --agent cursor [filter]            # Cursor only\n116: mise run test:e2e --agent factoryai-droid [filter]   # Factory AI Droid only\n117: mise run test:e2e --agent copilot-cli [filter]       # Copilot CLI only\n118: mise run test:e2e --agent pi [filter]                # Pi only\n119: ```\n120: \n121: E2E tests:\n122: \n123: - Use the `//go:build e2e` build tag\n124: - Located in `e2e/tests/`\n125: - See [`e2e/README.md`](e2e/README.md) for full documentation (structure, debugging, adding agents)\n126: - Test real agent interactions (Claude Code, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Cursor, Factory AI Droid, Copilot CLI, Pi, or Vogon creating files, committing, etc.)\n127: - Validate checkpoint scenarios documented in `docs/architecture/checkpoint-scenarios.md`\n128: - Support multiple agents via `E2E_AGENT` env var (`claude-code`, `gemini`, `opencode`, `cursor`, `factoryai-droid`, `copilot-cli`, `pi`, `vogon`)\n129: \n130: **Environment variables:**\n131: \n132: - `E2E_AGENT` - Agent to test with (default: `claude-code`)\n133: - `E2E_CLAUDE_MODEL` - Claude model to use (default: `haiku` for cost efficiency)\n134: - `E2E_TIMEOUT` - Timeout per prompt (default: `2m`)\n135: \n136: ### Test Parallelization\n137: \n138: **Always use `t.Parallel()` in tests.** Every top-level test function and subtest should call `t.Parallel()` unless it modifies process-global state (e.g., `os.Chdir()`).\n139: \n140: ```go\n141: func TestFeature_Foo(t *testing.T) {\n142:     t.Parallel()\n143:     // ...\n144: }\n145: \n146: // Integration tests with TestEnv\n147: func TestFeature_Bar(t *testing.T) {\n148:     t.Parallel()\n149:     env := NewFeatureBranchEnv(t)\n150:     // ...\n151: }\n152: ```\n153: \n154: **Exception:** Tests that modify process-global state cannot be parallelized. This includes `os.Chdir()`/`t.Chdir()` and `os.Setenv()`/`t.Setenv()` — Go's test framework will panic if these are used after `t.Parallel()`.\n155: \n156: ### Git in Tests\n157: \n158: **Tests that touch git state must use an isolated temp repo — never the real repo CWD.**\n159: \n160: Many handlers (lifecycle, strategy, hooks) resolve the git repo from CWD via `OpenRepository`, `GetGitCommonDir`, `DetectFileChanges`, etc. Without isolation, tests can create session state files, shadow branches, or other artifacts in the real `.git/` directory.\n161: \n162: Use the `testutil` helpers:\n163: \n164: ```go\n165: tmpDir := t.TempDir()\n166: testutil.InitRepo(t, tmpDir)                    // git init + user config + disable GPG\n167: testutil.WriteFile(t, tmpDir, \"f.txt\", \"init\")  // create a file\n168: testutil.GitAdd(t, tmpDir, \"f.txt\")             // stage it\n169: testutil.GitCommit(t, tmpDir, \"init\")           // commit (needs at least one commit for HEAD)\n170: t.Chdir(tmpDir)                                 // redirect CWD-based git resolution\n171: ```\n172: \n173: `testutil.InitRepo` configures `user.name`, `user.email`, and disables GPG signing — safe for CI environments without global git config.\n174: \n175: **Prefer `testutil.InitRepo()` over direct `git.PlainInit()` in tests.** When a test in this repo needs an initialized repository, use `testutil.InitRepo(t, dir)` unless the test specifically needs lower-level initialization behavior that the helper cannot provide. Do not call `git.PlainInit()` directly and then create commits or run CLI git operations without also reproducing the helper's repo-local config.\n176: \n177: **Do NOT** shell out to `git init`/`git commit` directly without setting user config and `--no-gpg-sign`, and **do NOT** run lifecycle/strategy handlers from the real repo CWD in tests.\n178: \n179: ### Spawning subprocesses in tests (TTY detection)\n180: \n181: Tests that spawn the real `entire` or `git` binary need the child to be non-interactive so prompts don't hang on a developer terminal.\n182: \n183: `interactive.CanPromptInteractively()` resolves in this order:\n184: \n185: 1. `ENTIRE_TEST_TTY=1` → force interactive ON (any other non-empty value → force OFF).\n186: 2. `testing.Testing()` → false. In-process `go test` runs are non-interactive by default; no per-test `t.Setenv(\"ENTIRE_TEST_TTY\", \"0\")` is needed.\n187: 3. Agent sentinels (`GEMINI_CLI`, `COPILOT_CLI`, `PI_CODING_AGENT`, `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`) → false.\n188: 4. `CI=<non-empty-non-false>` → false.\n189: 5. `/dev/tty` probe.\n190: \n191: For subprocesses spawning the real `entire` binary (e2e, integration tests, `entire` calling itself from a hook), prefer `execx.NonInteractive` over env-var plumbing:\n192: \n193: ```go\n194: import \"github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/execx\"\n195: \n196: cmd := execx.NonInteractive(ctx, getTestBinary(), \"status\")\n197: cmd.Dir = repoDir\n198: out, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n199: ```\n200: \n201: `execx.NonInteractive` puts the child in a new session with no controlling terminal (`Setsid` on Unix, `DETACHED_PROCESS | CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP` on Windows), so the child's `/dev/tty` probe fails naturally. No env var required.\n202: \n203: `interactive.UnderTest()` returns true when `testing.Testing()` or `ENTIRE_TEST_TTY` is set — use it where code needs to skip a real-terminal operation even if `CanPromptInteractively()` returns true (e.g., reading from `/dev/tty` directly inside `askConfirmTTY`).\n204: \n205: ### Linting and Formatting\n206: \n207: ```bash\n208: mise run fmt && mise run lint\n209: ```\n210: \n211: `mise run fmt` can rewrite files. Treat `mise run fmt && mise run lint` as a single verification sequence: if formatting changes anything, run lint again on the formatted tree rather than assuming a previous lint result still applies.\n212: \n213: ### Before Every Commit (REQUIRED)\n214: \n215: **CI will fail if you skip these steps:**\n216: \n217: ```bash\n218: mise run check\n219: ```\n220: \n221: Equivalent expanded form:\n222: \n223: ```bash\n224: mise run fmt      # Format code (CI enforces gofmt)\n225: mise run lint     # Lint check (CI enforces golangci-lint)\n226: mise run test:ci  # Run all tests (unit + integration)\n227: ```\n228: \n229: `mise run check` runs the three commands above.\n230: \n231: Safety note: do not treat a clean `mise run lint` result as final unless it was run after the most recent `mise run fmt` pass.\n232: \n233: ### Before Any Push Or Remote Code Update (REQUIRED)\n234: \n235: Before pushing commits or otherwise sending code changes to any remote, run `mise run lint` on the current tree and ensure it passes. If `mise run fmt` changed files, rerun `mise run lint` on the formatted tree before pushing.\n236: \n237: **Common CI failures from skipping this:**\n238: \n239: - `gofmt` formatting differences → run `mise run fmt`\n240: - Lint errors → run `mise run lint` and fix issues\n241: - Test failures → run `mise run test` and fix\n242: \n243: ### Code Duplication Prevention\n244: \n245: Before implementing Go code, use `/go:discover-related` to find existing utilities and patterns that might be reusable.\n246: \n247: **Check for duplication:**\n248: \n249: ```bash\n250: mise run dup           # Comprehensive check (threshold 50) with summary\n251: mise run dup:staged    # Check only staged files\n252: mise run lint          # Normal lint includes dupl at threshold 75 (new issues only)\n253: mise run lint:full     # All issues at threshold 75\n254: ```\n255: \n256: **Tiered thresholds:**\n257: \n258: - **75 tokens** (lint/CI) - Blocks on serious duplication (~20+ lines)\n259: - **50 tokens** (dup) - Advisory, catches smaller patterns (~10+ lines)\n260: \n261: When duplication is found:\n262: \n263: 1. Check if a helper already exists in `common.go` or nearby utility files\n264: 2. If not, consider extracting the duplicated logic to a shared helper\n265: 3. If duplication is intentional (e.g., test setup), add a `//nolint:dupl` comment with explanation\n266: \n267: ## Code Patterns\n268: \n269: ### Error Handling\n270: \n271: The CLI uses a specific pattern for error output to avoid duplication between Cobra and main.go.\n272: \n273: **How it works:**\n274: \n275: - `root.go` sets `SilenceErrors: true` globally - Cobra never prints errors\n276: - `main.go` prints errors to stderr, unless the error is a `SilentError`\n277: - Commands return `NewSilentError(err)` when they've already printed a custom message\n278: \n279: **When to use `SilentError`:**\n280: Use `NewSilentError()` when you want to print a custom, user-friendly error message instead of the raw error:\n281: \n282: ```go\n283: // In a command's RunE function:\n284: if _, err := paths.WorktreeRoot(); err != nil {\n285:     cmd.SilenceUsage = true  // Don't show usage for prerequisite errors\n286:     fmt.Fprintln(cmd.ErrOrStderr(), \"Not a git repository. Please run 'entire enable' from within a git repository.\")\n287:     return NewSilentError(errors.New(\"not a git repository\"))\n288: }\n289: ```\n290: \n291: **When NOT to use `SilentError`:**\n292: For normal errors where the default error message is sufficient, just return the error directly. main.go will print it:\n293: \n294: ```go\n295: // Normal error - main.go will print \"unknown strategy: foo\"\n296: return fmt.Errorf(\"unknown strategy: %s\", name)\n297: ```\n298: \n299: **Key files:**\n300: \n301: - `errors.go` - Defines `SilentError` type and `NewSilentError()` constructor\n302: - `root.go` - Sets `SilenceErrors: true` on root command\n303: - `main.go` - Checks for `SilentError` before printing\n304: \n305: ### Settings\n306: \n307: All settings access should go through the `settings` package (`cmd/entire/cli/settings/`).\n308: \n309: **Why a separate package:**\n310: The `settings` package exists to avoid import cycles. The `cli` package imports `strategy`, so `strategy` cannot import `cli`. The `settings` package provides shared settings loading that both can use.\n311: \n312: **Usage:**\n313: \n314: ```go\n315: import \"github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire/cli/settings\"\n316: \n317: // Load full settings object\n318: s, err := settings.Load()\n319: if err != nil {\n320:     // handle error\n321: }\n322: if s.Enabled {\n323:     // ...\n324: }\n325: \n326: // Or use convenience functions\n327: if settings.IsSummarizeEnabled() {\n328:     // ...\n329: }\n330: ```\n331: \n332: **Do NOT:**\n333: \n334: - Read `.entire/settings.json` or `.entire/settings.local.json` directly with `os.ReadFile`\n335: - Duplicate settings parsing logic in other packages\n336: - Create new settings helpers without adding them to the `settings` package\n337: \n338: **Key files:**\n339: \n340: - `settings/settings.go` - `EntireSettings` struct, `Load()`, and helper methods\n341: - `config.go` - Higher-level config functions that use settings (for `cli` package consumers)\n342: \n343: ### Logging vs User Output\n344: \n345: - **Internal/debug logging**: Use `logging.Debug/Info/Warn/Error(ctx, msg, attrs...)` from `cmd/entire/cli/logging/`. Writes to `.entire/logs/`.\n346: - **Enabling debug/perf logs locally**: Prefer adding `\"log_level\": \"DEBUG\"` to `.entire/settings.local.json` when you need detailed hook/perf logs. This file is gitignored, so it is a low-risk local-only change. `ENTIRE_LOG_LEVEL=debug` also works and takes precedence.\n347: - **User-facing output**: Use `fmt.Fprint*(cmd.OutOrStdout(), ...)` or `cmd.ErrOrStderr()`.\n348: \n349: Don't use `fmt.Print*` for operational messages (checkpoint saves, hook invocations, strategy decisions) - those should use the `logging` package.\n350: \n351: **Privacy**: Don't log user content (prompts, file contents, commit messages). Log only operational metadata (IDs, counts, paths, durations).\n352: \n353: ### Git Operations\n354: \n355: We use github.com/go-git/go-git for most git operations, but with important exceptions:\n356: \n357: #### go-git v5 Bugs - Use CLI Instead\n358: \n359: **Do NOT use go-git v5 for `checkout` or `reset --hard` operations.**\n360: \n361: go-git v5 has a bug where `worktree.Reset()` with `git.HardReset` and `worktree.Checkout()` incorrectly delete untracked directories even when they're listed in `.gitignore`. This would destroy `.entire/` and `.worktrees/` directories.\n362: \n363: Use the git CLI instead:\n364: \n365: ```go\n366: // WRONG - go-git deletes ignored directories\n367: worktree.Reset(&git.ResetOptions{\n368:     Commit: hash,\n369:     Mode:   git.HardReset,\n370: })\n371: \n372: // CORRECT - use git CLI\n373: cmd := exec.CommandContext(ctx, \"git\", \"reset\", \"--hard\", hash.String())\n374: ```\n375: \n376: See `HardResetWithProtection()` in `common.go` and `CheckoutBranch()` in `git_operations.go` for examples.\n377: \n378: Regression tests in `hard_reset_test.go` verify this behavior - if go-git v6 fixes this issue, those tests can be used to validate switching back.\n379: \n380: #### Repo Root vs Current Working Directory\n381: \n382: **Always use repo root (not `os.Getwd()`) when working with git-relative paths.**\n383: \n384: Git commands like `git status` and `worktree.Status()` return paths relative to the **repository root**, not the current working directory. When an agent runs from a subdirectory (e.g., `/repo/frontend`), using `os.Getwd()` to construct absolute paths will produce incorrect results for files in sibling directories.\n385: \n386: ```go\n387: // WRONG - breaks when running from subdirectory\n388: cwd, _ := os.Getwd()  // e.g., /repo/frontend\n389: absPath := filepath.Join(cwd, file)  // file=\"api/src/types.ts\" → /repo/frontend/api/src/types.ts (WRONG)\n390: \n391: // CORRECT - use repo root\n392: repoRoot, _ := paths.WorktreeRoot()\n393: absPath := filepath.Join(repoRoot, file)  // → /repo/api/src/types.ts (CORRECT)\n394: ```\n395: \n396: This also affects path filtering. The `paths.ToRelativePath()` function rejects paths starting with `..`, so computing relative paths from cwd instead of repo root will filter out files in sibling directories:\n397: \n398: ```go\n399: // WRONG - filters out sibling directory files\n400: cwd, _ := os.Getwd()  // /repo/frontend\n401: relPath := paths.ToRelativePath(\"/repo/api/file.ts\", cwd)  // returns \"\" (filtered out as \"../api/file.ts\")\n402: \n403: // CORRECT - keeps all repo files\n404: repoRoot, _ := paths.WorktreeRoot()\n405: relPath := paths.ToRelativePath(\"/repo/api/file.ts\", repoRoot)  // returns \"api/file.ts\"\n406: ```\n407: \n408: **When to use `os.Getwd()`:** Only when you actually need the current directory (e.g., finding agent session directories that are cwd-relative).\n409: \n410: **When to use repo root:** Any time you're working with paths from git status, git diff, or any git-relative file list.\n411: \n412: Test case in `state_test.go`: `TestFilterAndNormalizePaths_SiblingDirectories` documents this bug pattern.\n413: \n414: ### Session Strategy (`cmd/entire/cli/strategy/`)\n415: \n416: The CLI uses a manual-commit strategy for managing session data and checkpoints. The strategy implements the `Strategy` interface defined in `strategy.go`.\n417: \n418: #### Strategy Interface\n419: \n420: The `Strategy` interface provides:\n421: \n422: - `SaveStep()` - Save session step checkpoint (code + metadata)\n423: - `SaveTaskStep()` - Save subagent task step checkpoint\n424: - `GetRewindPoints()` / `Rewind()` - List and restore to checkpoints\n425: - `GetSessionLog()` / `GetSessionInfo()` - Retrieve session data\n426: - `ListSessions()` / `GetSession()` - Session discovery\n427: \n428: #### How It Works\n429: \n430: The manual-commit strategy (`manual_commit*.go`) does not modify the active branch - no commits are created on the working branch. Instead it:\n431: \n432: - Creates shadow branch `entire/<HEAD-commit-hash[:7]>-<worktreeHash[:6]>` per base commit + worktree\n433: - **Worktree-specific branches** - each git worktree gets its own shadow branch namespace, preventing conflicts\n434: - **Supports multiple concurrent sessions** - checkpoints from different sessions in the same directory interleave on the same shadow branch\n435: - Condenses session logs to permanent `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch on user commits\n436: - Uses the `post-rewrite` Git hook to keep local session linkage aligned after amend/rebase rewrites\n437: - Builds git trees in-memory using go-git plumbing APIs\n438: - Rewind restores files from shadow branch commit tree (does not use `git reset`)\n439: - **Location-independent transcript resolution** - transcript paths are always computed dynamically from the current repo location (via `agent.GetSessionDir` + `agent.ResolveSessionFile`), never stored in checkpoint metadata. This ensures restore/rewind works after repo relocation or across machines.\n440: - **Copilot token scoping** - Copilot CLI `session.shutdown` contains session-wide token aggregates. Checkpoint metadata must stay scoped to `CheckpointTranscriptStart`; condensation may separately backfill full-session Copilot totals into session state for `entire status`.\n441: - Tracks session state in `.git/entire-sessions/` (shared across worktrees)\n442: - **Shadow branch migration** - if user does stash/pull/rebase (HEAD changes without commit), shadow branch is automatically moved to new base commit\n443: - **Orphaned branch cleanup** - if a shadow branch exists without a corresponding session state file, it is automatically reset when a new session starts\n444: - PrePush hook can push `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch alongside user pushes\n445: - Safe to use on main/master since it never modifies commit history\n446: \n447: #### Key Files\n448: \n449: - `strategy.go` - Interface definition and context structs (`StepContext`, `TaskStepContext`, `RewindPoint`, etc.)\n450: - `common.go` - Helpers for metadata extraction, tree building, rewind validation, `ListCheckpoints()`\n451: - `session.go` - Session/checkpoint data structures\n452: - `push_common.go` - PrePush logic for pushing `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch\n453: - `manual_commit.go` - Manual-commit strategy main implementation\n454: - `manual_commit_types.go` - Type definitions: `SessionState`, `CheckpointInfo`, `CondenseResult`\n455: - `manual_commit_session.go` - Session state management (load/save/list session states)\n456: - `manual_commit_condensation.go` - Condense logic for copying logs to `entire/checkpoints/v1`\n457: - `manual_commit_rewind.go` - Rewind implementation: file restoration from checkpoint trees\n458: - `manual_commit_git.go` - Git operations: checkpoint commits, tree building\n459: - `manual_commit_logs.go` - Session log retrieval and session listing\n460: - `manual_commit_hooks.go` - Git hook handlers (prepare-commit-msg, post-commit, post-rewrite, pre-push)\n461: - `manual_commit_reset.go` - Shadow branch reset/cleanup functionality\n462: - `cleanup.go` - Cleanup discovery/deletion, including archived v2 generation retention\n463: - `generation_repair.go` - Archived v2 generation metadata repair from raw transcript timestamps\n464: - `session_state.go` - Package-level session state functions (`LoadSessionState`, `SaveSessionState`, `ListSessionStates`, `FindMostRecentSession`)\n465: - `hooks.go` - Git hook installation\n466: \n467: #### Checkpoint Package (`cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/`)\n468: \n469: - `checkpoint.go` - Data types (`Checkpoint`, `TemporaryCheckpoint`, `CommittedCheckpoint`)\n470: - `store.go` - `GitStore` struct wrapping git repository\n471: - `temporary.go` - Shadow branch operations (`WriteTemporary`, `ReadTemporary`, `ListTemporary`)\n472: - `committed.go` - Metadata branch operations (`WriteCommitted`, `ReadCommitted`, `ListCommitted`)\n473: \n474: #### Session Package (`cmd/entire/cli/session/`)\n475: \n476: - `session.go` - Session data types and interfaces\n477: - `state.go` - `StateStore` for managing `.git/entire-sessions/` files\n478: - `phase.go` - Session phase state machine (phases, events, transitions, actions)\n479: \n480: #### Session Phase State Machine\n481: \n482: Sessions track their lifecycle through phases managed by a state machine in `session/phase.go`:\n483: \n484: **Phases:** `ACTIVE`, `IDLE`, `ENDED`\n485: \n486: **Events:**\n487: \n488: - `TurnStart` - Agent begins a turn (UserPromptSubmit hook)\n489: - `TurnEnd` - Agent finishes a turn (Stop hook)\n490: - `GitCommit` - A git commit was made (PostCommit hook)\n491: - `SessionStart` - New session started\n492: - `SessionStop` - Session explicitly stopped\n493: \n494: **Key transitions:**\n495: \n496: - `IDLE + TurnStart → ACTIVE` - Agent starts working\n497: - `ACTIVE + TurnEnd → IDLE` - Agent finishes turn\n498: - `ACTIVE + GitCommit → ACTIVE` - User commits while agent is working (condense immediately)\n499: - `IDLE + GitCommit → IDLE` - User commits between turns (condense immediately)\n500: - `ENDED + GitCommit → ENDED` - Post-session commit (condense if files touched)\n501: \n502: The state machine emits **actions** (e.g., `ActionCondense`, `ActionUpdateLastInteraction`) that hook handlers dispatch to strategy-specific implementations.\n503: \n504: #### Metadata Structure\n505: \n506: **Shadow branches** (`entire/<commit-hash[:7]>-<worktreeHash[:6]>`):\n507: \n508: ```\n509: .entire/metadata/<session-id>/\n510: ├── full.jsonl               # Session transcript\n511: ├── prompt.txt               # Checkpoint-scoped user prompts\n512: └── tasks/<tool-use-id>/     # Task checkpoints\n513:     ├── checkpoint.json      # UUID mapping for rewind\n514:     └── agent-<id>.jsonl     # Subagent transcript\n515: ```\n516: \n517: **Metadata branch** (`entire/checkpoints/v1`) - sharded checkpoint format:\n518: \n519: ```\n520: <checkpoint-id[:2]>/<checkpoint-id[2:]>/\n521: ├── metadata.json            # CheckpointSummary (aggregated stats)\n522: ├── 0/                       # First session (0-based indexing)\n523: │   ├── metadata.json        # Session-specific metadata\n524: │   ├── full.jsonl           # Session transcript\n525: │   ├── prompt.txt           # Checkpoint-scoped user prompts\n526: │   ├── content_hash.txt     # SHA256 of transcript\n527: │   └── tasks/<tool-use-id>/ # Task checkpoints (if applicable)\n528: │       ├── checkpoint.json  # UUID mapping\n529: │       └── agent-<id>.jsonl # Subagent transcript\n530: ├── 1/                       # Second session (if multiple sessions)\n531: │   ├── metadata.json\n532: │   ├── full.jsonl\n533: │   └── ...\n534: └── ...\n535: ```\n536: \n537: **Multi-session metadata.json format:**\n538: \n539: ```json\n540: {\n541:   \"checkpoint_id\": \"abc123def456\",\n542:   \"session_id\": \"2026-01-13-uuid\", // Current/latest session\n543:   \"session_ids\": [\"2026-01-13-uuid1\", \"2026-01-13-uuid2\"], // All sessions\n544:   \"session_count\": 2, // Number of sessions in this checkpoint\n545:   \"strategy\": \"manual-commit\",\n546:   \"created_at\": \"2026-01-13T12:00:00Z\",\n547:   \"files_touched\": [\"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"] // Merged from all sessions\n548: }\n549: ```\n550: \n551: When multiple sessions are condensed to the same checkpoint (same base commit):\n552: \n553: - Sessions are stored in numbered subfolders using 0-based indexing (`0/`, `1/`, `2/`, etc.)\n554: - Latest session is always in the highest-numbered folder\n555: - `session_ids` array tracks all sessions, `session_count` increments\n556: \n557: **Session State** (filesystem, `.git/entire-sessions/`):\n558: \n559: ```\n560: <session-id>.json            # Active session state (base_commit, checkpoint_count, etc.)\n561: ```\n562: \n563: #### Checkpoint ID Linking\n564: \n565: The strategy uses a **12-hex-char random checkpoint ID** (e.g., `a3b2c4d5e6f7`) as the stable identifier linking user commits to metadata.\n566: \n567: **How checkpoint IDs work:**\n568: \n569: 1. **Generated once per checkpoint**: When condensing session metadata to the metadata branch\n570: \n571: 2. **Added to user commits** via `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer:\n572:    - **Manual-commit**: Added via `prepare-commit-msg` hook (user can remove it before committing)\n573: \n574: 3. **Used for directory sharding** on `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch:\n575:    - Path format: `<id[:2]>/<id[2:]>/`\n576:    - Example: `a3b2c4d5e6f7` → `a3/b2c4d5e6f7/`\n577:    - Creates 256 shards to avoid directory bloat\n578: \n579: 4. **Appears in commit subject** on `entire/checkpoints/v1` commits:\n580:    - Format: `Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7`\n581:    - Makes `git log entire/checkpoints/v1` readable and searchable\n582: \n583: **Bidirectional linking:**\n584: \n585: ```\n586: User commit → Metadata:\n587:   Extract \"Entire-Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7\" trailer\n588:   → Read a3/b2c4d5e6f7/ directory from entire/checkpoints/v1 tree at HEAD\n589: \n590: Metadata → User commits:\n591:   Given checkpoint ID a3b2c4d5e6f7\n592:   → Search user branch history for commits with \"Entire-Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7\" trailer\n593: ```\n594: \n595: Note: Commit subjects on `entire/checkpoints/v1` (e.g., `Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7`) are\n596: for human readability in `git log` only. The CLI always reads from the tree at HEAD.\n597: \n598: **Example:**\n599: \n600: ```\n601: User's commit (on main branch):\n602:   \"Implement login feature\n603: \n604:   Entire-Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7\"\n605:        ↓ ↑\n606:        Linked via checkpoint ID\n607:        ↓ ↑\n608: entire/checkpoints/v1 commit:\n609:   Subject: \"Checkpoint: a3b2c4d5e6f7\"\n610: \n611:   Tree: a3/b2c4d5e6f7/\n612:     ├── metadata.json (checkpoint_id: \"a3b2c4d5e6f7\")\n613:     ├── full.jsonl (session transcript)\n614:     └── prompt.txt\n615: ```\n616: \n617: #### Commit Trailers\n618: \n619: **On user's active branch commits:**\n620: \n621: - `Entire-Checkpoint: <checkpoint-id>` - 12-hex-char ID linking to metadata on `entire/checkpoints/v1`\n622:   - Added via `prepare-commit-msg` hook; user can remove it before committing to skip linking\n623: \n624: **On shadow branch commits (`entire/<commit-hash[:7]>-<worktreeHash[:6]>`):**\n625: \n626: - `Entire-Session: <session-id>` - Session identifier\n627: - `Entire-Metadata: <path>` - Path to metadata directory within the tree\n628: - `Entire-Task-Metadata: <path>` - Path to task metadata directory (for task checkpoints)\n629: - `Entire-Strategy: manual-commit` - Strategy that created the commit\n630: \n631: **On metadata branch commits (`entire/checkpoints/v1`):**\n632: \n633: Commit subject: `Checkpoint: <checkpoint-id>` (or custom subject for task checkpoints)\n634: \n635: Trailers:\n636: \n637: - `Entire-Session: <session-id>` - Session identifier\n638: - `Entire-Strategy: <strategy>` - Strategy name (manual-commit)\n639: - `Entire-Agent: <agent-name>` - Agent name (optional, e.g., \"Claude Code\")\n640: - `Ephemeral-branch: <branch>` - Shadow branch name (optional)\n641: - `Entire-Metadata-Task: <path>` - Task metadata path (optional, for task checkpoints)\n642: \n643: **Note:** The strategy keeps active branch history clean - the only addition to user commits is the single `Entire-Checkpoint` trailer. It never creates commits on the active branch (the user creates them manually). All detailed session data (transcripts, prompts, context) is stored on the `entire/checkpoints/v1` orphan branch or shadow branches.\n644: \n645: #### Multi-Session Behavior\n646: \n647: **Concurrent Sessions:**\n648: \n649: - When a second session starts in the same directory while another has uncommitted checkpoints, a warning is shown\n650: - Both sessions can proceed - their checkpoints interleave on the same shadow branch\n651: - Each session's `RewindPoint` includes `SessionID` and `SessionPrompt` to help identify which checkpoint belongs to which session\n652: - On commit, all sessions are condensed together with archived sessions in numbered subfolders\n653: - Note: Different git worktrees have separate shadow branches (worktree-specific naming), so concurrent sessions in different worktrees do not conflict\n654: \n655: **Orphaned Shadow Branches:**\n656: \n657: - A shadow branch is \"orphaned\" if it exists but has no corresponding session state file\n658: - This can happen if the state file is manually deleted or lost\n659: - When a new session starts with an orphaned branch, the branch is automatically reset\n660: - If the existing session DOES have a state file (concurrent session in same directory), a `SessionIDConflictError` is returned\n661: \n662: **Shadow Branch Migration (Pull/Rebase):**\n663: \n664: - If user does stash → pull → apply (or rebase), HEAD changes but work isn't committed\n665: - The shadow branch would be orphaned at the old commit\n666: - Detection: base commit changed AND old shadow branch still exists (would be deleted if user committed)\n667: - Action: shadow branch is renamed from `entire/<old-hash>-<worktreeHash>` to `entire/<new-hash>-<worktreeHash>`\n668: - Session continues seamlessly with checkpoints preserved\n669: \n670: #### When Modifying the Strategy\n671: \n672: - The strategy must implement the full `Strategy` interface\n673: - Test with `mise run test` - strategy tests are in `*_test.go` files\n674: - **Update both CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md** when modifying the strategy to keep documentation current\n675: \n676: ### `entire review` Command\n677: \n678: `entire review` runs a set of configured review skills inside an agent session. The review session is an immutable fact attached to a checkpoint — no verdict, no status tracking, no empty commits. On the next `git commit`, the review session is condensed into the checkpoint metadata alongside normal sessions, permanently recording that the code was reviewed and which skills were run.\n679: \n680: #### Command Surface\n681: \n682: ```\n683: entire review                          # Normal run: load config, run configured agent(s)\n684: entire review --edit                   # Re-open the skills picker before running\n685: entire review --agent <name>           # Force a specific configured agent (skips multi-picker)\n686: entire review attach <session-id>      # Tag an existing agent session as a review (post-hoc)\n687: entire review attach --force           # Skip confirmation\n688: entire review attach --agent <name>    # Agent that created the session\n689: entire review attach --skills <s,...>  # Declare which skills were run\n690: ```\n691: \n692: When two or more launchable agents are configured and `--agent` is not set, a multi-select picker appears with an optional per-run prompt field (e.g. \"focus on security\"). Selecting one agent or passing `--agent` runs the single-agent path; selecting two or more runs the N-agent path.\n693: \n694: #### Settings Schema\n695: \n696: Review skills are configured per-agent in `.entire/settings.json`:\n697: \n698: ```json\n699: {\n700:   \"review\": {\n701:     \"claude-code\": {\"skills\": [\"/pr-review-toolkit:review-pr\"], \"prompt\": \"Be thorough.\"},\n702:     \"codex\": {\"skills\": [\"/codex:adversarial-review\"]}\n703:   }\n704: }\n705: ```\n706: \n707: The key is the agent name. The value is a `ReviewConfig` with `skills` (skill invocations passed verbatim to the agent) and optional `prompt` (an always-prompt appended to the composed prompt). Settings field: `EntireSettings.Review` in `cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go`.\n708: \n709: #### How It Works (env-var handshake)\n710: \n711: 1. `entire review` selects the configured agent (override → alphabetically first → prompt if multiple), composes the review prompt via `review.ComposeReviewPrompt`, and computes scope (closest-ancestor branch via `review.ComputeScopeStats`).\n712: 2. **For launchable agents** (claude-code, codex, gemini-cli): the spawned agent process is given env vars `ENTIRE_REVIEW_{SESSION,AGENT,SKILLS,PROMPT,STARTING_SHA}` that the agent's `UserPromptSubmit` lifecycle hook reads to tag the session as `Kind = \"agent_review\"` with the configured skills/prompt. Each spawned process has its own env, so multiple worktrees and multi-agent runs are correct by construction (no shared marker file, no race).\n713: 3. **For non-launchable agents** (cursor, opencode, factoryai-droid): `RunMarkerFallback` writes a `PendingReviewMarker` file and prints guidance — the user opens the agent themselves and runs the skills. Single shared file (`review/marker_fallback.go`); adding new non-launchable agents is a registry entry, not a new file.\n714: 4. The agent runs the review skills; the session ends naturally.\n715: 5. On the next `git commit`, the PostCommit hook condenses the review session into the checkpoint on `entire/checkpoints/v1`, with `Kind` and `ReviewSkills` recorded in `CommittedMetadata`.\n716: 6. The `CheckpointSummary` sets `HasReview = true` for O(1) lookup. `HasReview` is an umbrella \"any review happened\" flag — future review kinds (e.g. manual review) should also set it.\n717: 7. `entire status` and the re-run guard read `HasReview` from the checkpoint metadata (no commit history walking).\n718: \n719: #### Checkpoint Metadata\n720: \n721: Review metadata is stored at two levels on `entire/checkpoints/v1`:\n722: \n723: - **`CommittedMetadata` (per-session)**: `kind: \"agent_review\"`, `review_skills: [\"/skill1\", \"/skill2\"]`, `review_prompt: \"...\"`\n724: - **`CheckpointSummary` (per-checkpoint)**: `has_review: true` (umbrella; set when any session in the checkpoint has a review-kind `Kind`)\n725: \n726: #### Architecture\n727: \n728: - **`AgentReviewer` interface** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/types/reviewer.go`): per-agent contract with `Name() string` and `Start(ctx, RunConfig) (Process, error)`. Each launchable agent implements this in its own package.\n729: - **`ReviewerTemplate`** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/types/template.go`): shared scaffolding (Spawn → pipe stdout → run parser → forward events → close). Each agent supplies only its `BuildCmd` (argv/env) and `Parser` (stdout-to-Event stream).\n730: - **`Sink` interface**: consumers of the event stream. Production sinks: `DumpSink` (post-run per-agent narrative), `TUISink` (Bubble Tea live dashboard with Ctrl+O drill-in), `SynthesisSink` (opt-in y/N cross-agent verdict). Sinks are composed by `composeMultiAgentSinks` based on TTY detection.\n731: - **`Run(ctx, reviewer, cfg, sinks)`** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/run.go`): single-agent orchestrator. Forwards events to all sinks via `AgentEvent`, calls `RunFinished` once at end with a populated `RunSummary`. Sink dispatch is serialized; sinks need not internally synchronize.\n732: - **`RunMulti(ctx, reviewers, cfg, sinks)`** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/run_multi.go`): N-agent orchestrator. Each agent runs concurrently in its own goroutine; events fan into a single dispatch loop so the serial-dispatch contract is preserved. Per-agent skills/prompts are injected via `perAgentConfiguredReviewer` adapter (each reviewer sees its own `RunConfig` despite the shared API surface).\n733: - **Env-var contract** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go`): single source of truth for `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` constants used by spawn-side and lifecycle adoption.\n734: - **Scope detection** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/scope.go`): `detectScopeBaseRef` finds the closest non-self ancestor branch by tip timestamp, with fallback chain `origin/HEAD → origin/main → origin/master → main → master`. Banner output: \"Reviewing feat/X vs main: 3 commits, 7 files changed, 2 uncommitted\".\n735: \n736: #### Multi-Agent UI\n737: \n738: When `RunMulti` is dispatched in a TTY, the sink slice is `[TUISink, DumpSink, SynthesisSink?]`:\n739: \n740: - **`TUISink` / `reviewTUIModel`** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_sink.go`, `tui_model.go`, `tui_detail.go`): live dashboard with one row per agent (name, status, tokens, last assistant preview, duration). `Ctrl+O` enters drill-in mode on the alt screen showing the full event buffer for the selected agent; `Esc` returns to the dashboard. `Ctrl+C` cancels the run via the shared `CancelFunc`. The model uses `tea.WithoutSignalHandler` so the cobra root retains SIGINT routing. After all agents finish, the user dismisses with any key — `RunFinished` blocks on dismissal so `DumpSink` renders below the TUI rather than overlapping it.\n741: - **`SynthesisSink`** (`cmd/entire/cli/review/synthesis_sink.go`): opt-in y/N prompt offered after the dump. On \"y\", composes a synthesis prompt covering all agent narratives + per-run user prompt, calls the configured summary provider, and prints the unified verdict. Skipped silently when stdin can't prompt, the run was cancelled, or fewer than 2 agents produced usable output. Provider failures degrade gracefully (\"synthesis unavailable: <err>\") so the user can still commit.\n742: - **Sink composition** (`composeMultiAgentSinks` in `cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go`): pure helper taking explicit `isTTY`/`canPrompt` so tests don't depend on real TTY detection. `findTUISink` picks the TUI out of the slice for `Start`/`Wait` lifecycle hooks.\n743: \n744: #### Skill Discovery (Claude Code)\n745: \n746: `DiscoverReviewSkills` (`cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/discovery.go`) walks three roots: plugin cache (`~/.claude/plugins/cache/<market>/<plugin>/<version>/{skills,commands,agents}`), user skills (`~/.claude/skills`), user commands/agents (`~/.claude/commands`, `~/.claude/agents`).\n747: \n748: For the plugin cache, `pickLatestVersion` picks ONE version directory per plugin: highest valid semver wins; if no entries parse as semver, the lexicographic max is picked (handles the `unknown` sentinel some plugins ship). Without this, multiple installed versions of a plugin produced duplicate skill entries in the picker and prompt.\n749: \n750: #### Anti-Features (do NOT recreate)\n751: \n752: The redesign eliminated several constructs from the prior implementation. None should be reintroduced without explicit design:\n753: \n754: - `PendingReviewMarker` for launchable agents (env-var handshake makes it unnecessary)\n755: - `WorktreePath` field + worktree-scoping logic (env per process eliminates the multi-tenant problem)\n756: - `AgentEntries` map on the marker (each agent has its own env)\n757: - Marker overwrite tripwire / refuse-attach guard (the bug classes they defended against don't exist)\n758: - `--track-only` flag (intentionally removed by #1009)\n759: - `--postreview` / `--finalize` / empty review commits / `/entire-review:finish` skill installer\n760: - `Launcher` + `HeadlessLauncher` as separate interfaces (single `AgentReviewer`)\n761: - `filterCodexOutput` in shared multi-agent code (lives in codex's adapter)\n762: - `sync.Once`-guarded onCancel + parallel `signal.Notify` goroutine (single cancel from start)\n763: \n764: #### Key Files\n765: \n766: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/cmd.go` — `NewCommand()`, `runReview` dispatch fork, `composeMultiAgentSinks`\n767: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/picker.go` / `multipicker.go` — config-edit picker, first-run setup, single- and multi-agent selection\n768: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/attach.go` + `cli/review_helpers.go:newReviewAttachCmd` — `entire review attach` subcommand\n769: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/marker_fallback.go` — non-launchable agent flow (single shared file)\n770: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/prompt.go` / `scope.go` / `run.go` / `dump.go` / `run_multi.go` — core machinery (single-agent + N-agent fan-in)\n771: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/tui_sink.go` / `tui_model.go` / `tui_detail.go` — Bubble Tea TUI sink\n772: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/synthesis_sink.go` / `synthesis_prompt.go` — opt-in cross-agent verdict\n773: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/types/{reviewer,sink,template}.go` — interface contracts (CU2 + CU4 + CU5b)\n774: - `cmd/entire/cli/review/env.go` — `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` constants + `EncodeSkills`/`DecodeSkills` + `AppendReviewEnv`\n775: - `cmd/entire/cli/agent/{claudecode,codex,geminicli}/reviewer.go` — per-agent `AgentReviewer` implementations (claude-code, codex with chrome filter, gemini-cli)\n776: - `cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/discovery.go` — skill discovery + `pickLatestVersion` plugin-cache dedupe\n777: - `cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go` — `adoptReviewEnv` reads `ENTIRE_REVIEW_*` from process env; replaces marker-file adoption\n778: - `cmd/entire/cli/review_bridge.go` / `review_helpers.go` — bridge code in `cli` package for cycle-bound functions (`headHasReviewCheckpoint`, `launchableReviewerFor`, `newReviewAttachCmd`, `lazySynthesisProvider`)\n779: - `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/checkpoint.go` — `Kind`, `ReviewSkills`, `ReviewPrompt` on `CommittedMetadata`; `HasReview` on `CheckpointSummary`\n780: - `cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings.go` — `EntireSettings.Review` field\n781: \n782: # Important Notes\n783: \n784: - **Before committing:** Follow the \"Before Every Commit (REQUIRED)\" checklist above - CI will fail without it\n785: - Integration tests: run `mise run test:integration` when changing integration test code\n786: - When adding new features, ensure they are well-tested and documented.\n787: - Always check for code duplication and refactor as needed.\n788: \n789: ## Go Code Style\n790: \n791: - Write lint-compliant Go code on the first attempt. Before outputting Go code, mentally verify it passes `golangci-lint` (or your specific linter).\n792: - Follow standard Go idioms: proper error handling, no unused variables/imports, correct formatting (gofmt), meaningful names.\n793: - Handle all errors explicitly—don't leave them unchecked.\n794: - Reference `.golangci.yml` for enabled linters before writing Go code.\n795: \n796: ## Accessibility\n797: \n798: The CLI supports an accessibility mode for users who rely on screen readers. This mode uses simpler text prompts instead of interactive TUI elements.\n799: \n800: ### Environment Variable\n801: \n802: - `ACCESSIBLE=1` (or any non-empty value) enables accessibility mode\n803: - Users can set this in their shell profile (`.bashrc`, `.zshrc`) for persistent use\n804: \n805: ### Implementation Guidelines\n806: \n807: When adding new interactive forms or prompts using `huh`:\n808: \n809: **In the `cli` package:**\n810: Use `NewAccessibleForm()` instead of `huh.NewForm()`:\n811: \n812: ```go\n813: // Good - respects ACCESSIBLE env var\n814: form := NewAccessibleForm(\n815:     huh.NewGroup(\n816:         huh.NewSelect[string]().\n817:             Title(\"Choose an option\").\n818:             Options(...).\n819:             Value(&choice),\n820:     ),\n821: )\n822: \n823: // Bad - ignores accessibility setting\n824: form := huh.NewForm(...)\n825: ```\n826: \n827: **In the `strategy` package:**\n828: Use the `isAccessibleMode()` helper. Note that `WithAccessible()` is only available on forms, not individual fields, so wrap confirmations in a form:\n829: \n830: ```go\n831: form := huh.NewForm(\n832:     huh.NewGroup(\n833:         huh.NewConfirm().\n834:             Title(\"Confirm action?\").\n835:             Value(&confirmed),\n836:     ),\n837: )\n838: if isAccessibleMode() {\n839:     form = form.WithAccessible(true)\n840: }\n841: if err := form.Run(); err != nil { ... }\n842: ```\n843: \n844: ### Key Points\n845: \n846: - Always use the accessibility helpers for any `huh` forms/prompts\n847: - Test new interactive features with `ACCESSIBLE=1` to ensure they work\n848: - The accessible mode is documented in `--help` output\n\n(End of file - total 848 lines)\n</content>",
